@.str.27 = private unnamed_addr constant [1641 x i8] c"\0A      Download the specified URL, unpack it and return the path of the\0A      unpacked tree. The file must be a tape archive (`.tar`) compressed\0A      with `gzip`, `bzip2` or `xz`. The top-level path component of the\0A      files in the tarball is removed, so it is best if the tarball\0A      contains a single directory at top level. The typical use of the\0A      function is to obtain external Nix expression dependencies, such as\0A      a particular version of Nixpkgs, e.g.\0A\0A      ```nix\0A      with import (fetchTarball https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/archive/nixos-14.12.tar.gz) {};\0A\0A      stdenv.mkDerivation { \E2\80\A6 }\0A      ```\0A\0A      The fetched tarball is cached for a certain amount of time (1\0A      hour by default) in `~/.cache/nix/tarballs/`. You can change the\0A      cache timeout either on the command line with `--tarball-ttl`\0A      *number-of-seconds* or in the Nix configuration file by adding\0A      the line `tarball-ttl = ` *number-of-seconds*.\0A\0A      Note that when obtaining the hash with `nix-prefetch-url` the\0A      option `--unpack` is required.\0A\0A      This function can also verify the contents against a hash. In that\0A      case, the function takes a set instead of a URL. The set requires\0A      the attribute `url` and the attribute `sha256`, e.g.\0A\0A      ```nix\0A      with import (fetchTarball {\0A        url = \22https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/archive/nixos-14.12.tar.gz\22;\0A        sha256 = \221jppksrfvbk5ypiqdz4cddxdl8z6zyzdb2srq8fcffr327ld5jj2\22;\0A      }) {};\0A\0A      stdenv.mkDerivation { \E2\80\A6 }\0A      ```\0A\0A      Not available in [restricted evaluation mode](@docroot@/command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-restrict-eval).\0A    \00", align 1
